# Story
def explain_details():
    story = f"""
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{VERSION}
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Description

    Separate reads in fastq files by sample well groups.

    Typical use:

        fastq_sep_groups.py -f <R1.fastq.gz> -c <chemistry> -k <kit> -g [sample well groups]...

    -------------------
    Input fastq files should be gzip compressed. If only given R1 filename
        (via -f or --fq1), R2 is filename is deduced from that.

    -------------------
    The chemistry version and kit for the data must be given (via --chemistry and --kit).

    -------------------
    Sample well groups are specified by <name> and <wells>.

    Wells are specified individually or in blocks or ranges like this:
        'C4' specifies a single well.
        'A1:C6' specifies a block as [top-left]:[bottom-right]; 'A1-A6,B1-B6,C1-C6'
        'A1-B6' specifies a range as [start]-[end]; 'A1-A12,B1-6'
        Multiple selections are joined by commas (NO SPACES), e.g. 'A1-A6,B1:D3,C4'

    Valid group wells depend on kit
    If more than one 96-well plate is available for a barcoding round, this is
        specified with a "pN." prefix, where N is the plate number. For example,
        this block specifies 3 cols (4, 5, 6) across two plates: 'p1.A4:p2.H6'
        (16 rows, 48 total wells).
    If two barcode rounds are used for group specification, wells are specified
        for each round, and then joined via underscores; For example 'B5__A1:H3'

    Groups may be specified via command line or from a list file (--gfile <list>).

    For group specification via command line, each group requires one command:
        --group <name1> <wells-for-group1>
        --group <name2> <wells-for-group2>
        ... etc ...

    For group specification via file (--gfile <list>), a simple, space delimited
    text list file is used. Each file line should have:
        '<name1> <wells1>'
        '<name2> <wells2>'
        ... etc ...

    Groups cannot overlap.

    -------------------
    Header name checking makes sure fq1 and fq2 read header information matches.
        Specifically, <name> and <number> are extracted from fq1 and fq2 header
        lines and checked that 1) names match, and 2) that numbers from fq1 and fq2
        are 1 and 2, respectively. 
    The separator between items may differ among sequencing platforms. By default
        space is used, but slash may be specified via --slash_name_sep

        Illumina, Ultima, Element use space:  '@<name> <num>'
        BGI uses slash:                       '@<name>/<num>'

    -------------------
    Output fastq files go into 'output_dir/'. If not given, output name is based 
        on input fastq file (wihtout the path).
    New files named as group name with added prefix 'opref' (Default: '{DEF_OPREF}')

    -------------------
    Output compression.
    Default behavior is to output 'blocks' of per-group fastq files that are 
        compressed by a background process (preferably 'pigz'; 'gzip' if needed).
        This requires 'output_dir' to initially be absent (--force removes this)
    --no_back_gz Disables background compression, yielding uncompressed outputs.
    --write_gz Results in direct compression (Python gzip) 
    
    -------------------
    Misc defaults
        --bc_samp_slice {DEF_BC_SAMP_SLICE}
        --edit_dist {DEF_BC_EDIT_DIST}
"""

    print("XXX")

    # Set env var so that the screen is not cleared after pager exits
    os.environ["PAGER"] = "less -R -X"
    pydoc.pager(story)


